{- | The compiler's single-stage probe gates, as one runner over a table of
   rows.

   Six shell gates used to do this, one script per probe, each a near-copy of
   the same loop: spawn `test/bin/<entry>_main` over a corpus of `.mdk`
   files, strip the native runtime's trailing `()`, and diff the result
   against a committed golden sitting beside each fixture. The probe, the
   corpus and the golden's suffix are the data; the loop is not. The rows
   here name them:

   | retired gate | probe | corpus | golden |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | `diff_compiler_diagnostics` | (in-process: `compiler/driver/diagnostics_test.mdk`) | `resolve`/`exhaust`/`check_match` fixtures | `.analyze.golden` |
   | `diff_compiler_exhaust` | (in-process: `compiler/frontend/exhaust_test.mdk`) | `test/exhaust_fixtures` | `.expected` |
   | `diff_compiler_fmt` | `fmt_main` | `test/fmt_fixtures` + `test/parse_fixtures` | `.fmt.golden` |
   | `diff_compiler_lex_files` | `lex_main` | three lexer modules and twelve stdlib modules | `.lextok.golden` |
   | `diff_compiler_lint` | `lint_main --only=<rule>` | `test/lint_fixtures` | `.expected` |
   | `diff_compiler_lint_fix` | `lint_fix_main --only=<rule>` | `test/lint_fix_fixtures` | `.fixed` |

   `diff_compiler_lex_files`' corpus is REAL SOURCE, not fixtures: the
   lexer modules plus twelve stdlib modules, which exercise
   whatever real code actually uses (block comments, the full operator set)
   rather than what a curated corpus remembered to include.

   `diff_compiler_lint`'s probe reads an environment variable the other five
   do not: `lint_main` builds its stdlib index from `$MEDAKA_ROOT/stdlib`, and
   indexes NOTHING when that is unset, which silences every
   `rule-stdlib-reimpl` finding. `inline_suppression` answers differently
   without it and its golden is the with-index answer, so an unset
   `MEDAKA_ROOT` reds it rather than passing on an empty index.
   `medaka gate run` sets it for every gate (`gate_cmd.gateArgs`); a bare
   `medaka test --native` on this file inherits whatever the shell had.

   Eight more retired scripts do NOT fit `Row` — each spawns its probe more
   than once per fixture, with per-fixture routing, injected environment
   variables, stdin, or a comparison that is not a text diff, which one
   (probe, corpus, goldenSuffix) triple can't express. Each keeps its own
   block below instead, over the SAME two predicates as a `Row`: a spawn's
   exit code and stderr, and its stdout against a golden.

   - `diff_compiler_references_correctness` dumps a whole 1-4-module
     project per fixture via `refindex_main --dump`, not a per-file corpus
     loop, over twelve fixture directories.
   - `diff_compiler_effect_hole` / `diff_compiler_effect_param` spawn BOTH
     the native CLI (`./medaka check`) and `test/bin/check_main` over
     inline-heredoc fixtures, asserting on type-scheme substrings and
     rejection diagnostics rather than a single stdout diff.
   - `diff_compiler_typecheck_errors` routes each fixture to one or both of
     two drivers (`typecheck_main`, `check_main`) by a per-fixture
     "prelude-dependent" list — not expressible as one row's single probe.
   - `diff_compiler_repl` feeds a whole session transcript to its probe over
     STDIN, which no `Row` has a path for.
   - `diff_compiler_analyze_project` compares two whole-project diagnostic
     dumps SEMANTICALLY: per file, the MULTISET of `(severity, message)`
     pairs and the START position of each located diagnostic, against a
     frozen `oracle.json` that has no regenerator. Ordering is not part of
     the claim, so a text diff would report a reordering as a failure.
   - `diff_compiler_test` grades its corpus against per-module goldens AND
     an eleven-case battery over the same probe — exit codes, two whole
     pinned reports, and substrings that must and must not appear — which
     one row's single stdout diff cannot express.

   Two of the sixteen retired scripts are not corpus-and-golden shaped and
   are not bent into rows either — the two tests at the foot of this file:

   - `diff_compiler_new` scaffolded ONE project tree and compared the whole
     TREE (file list and bytes) against `test/new_golden/myapp`. There is no
     corpus and no per-fixture golden, and `test/capture_goldens.sh new` is a
     stale no-op, so a mismatch is reviewed and hand-copied rather than
     captured.
   - `diff_compiler_entry_exit_codes` pins #440: five error paths through the
     emitter probe's `driveModules`, each asserted on the exit code AND on
     stdout staying empty AND on stderr carrying a diagnostic, plus a control
     proving those five discriminate. Its inputs are synthesized, three of
     them being a file that must NOT exist, which no corpus can hold.

   The registry entry keeps `diff_compiler_fmt`'s name so the merged gate
   inherits that gate's measured cost history rather than arriving uncosted
   (`gate_cost.baselineKey` reads the `run` path, and `medaka gate balance`
   hard-refuses on a gate with no cost sample). Each retired gate's own
   baseline row is dropped in the commit that retires it, so the baseline
   holds no row the registry cannot key — `test/gate_cost_ingest.sh`'s
   `--registry` pass would drop it anyway, a release later. A failure names
   the RETIRED gate it belongs to, so the shared registry name never hides
   which property broke.

   Every row also grades what the scripts did not: the probe's exit code and
   its stderr, not stdout alone (`probe_runner.processFailure`). Runs under
   `medaka test --native`.

   Every block below also writes down how many units it grades, checked by
   `probe_runner.countFloor` — the non-row half of what `Row.fixtures` does.
   A corpus that grew reds until the number beside it is updated to match,
   which is the price of a corpus that shrinks reding too. -}

